{"analysis":"The experiment successfully computed the missense/silent mutation ratio per patient, merged it with clinical survival data, and conducted both Cox proportional hazards modeling and Kaplan–Meier analysis.

Cox PH model (adjusted for age and gender) showed that a higher missense/silent ratio is significantly associated with increased hazard of death (HR = 1.43, 95% CI 1.04–1.97, p = 0.030), supporting the hypothesis.  

Kaplan–Meier curves for the lowest vs highest tertiles of the ratio illustrated poorer survival in the high‐ratio group, but the log‐rank test did not reach statistical significance (χ² = 1.816, p = 0.178).","success":true}
